{% markdown %}

**Figma kits often tell a design system's story a second time.** Designers draw the components, engineers build them, and the two versions drift because nothing ties one to the other. Every correction gets made twice, by different people, or often doesn't and the two worlds drift. Double the work and half the benefit.

**UNDRR's Mangrove Figma library is derived instead: a change made once lands everywhere.** When we changed a core spacing value in code and republished, a designer's pilot file updated and kept every one of their edits. Nobody redrew anything or compared design and code by eye.

**That works because three sources already agree on how truth is told.** The content rules (structured content schemas) say what each component contains, the brand settings (design tokens) say how it looks in each of UNDRR's five brands, and the developers' live component catalogue ([Storybook](https://mangrove.undrr.org/)) says how it is laid out. A plugin I built reads all three and generates a native Figma library: 297 variants in 25 component families, real components rather than pictures of them.

**That is triangulation: each source answers one question.** So when Figma disagreed with one of them, I knew which, and every mismatch became a bug with an address instead of an argument about taste.

{% endmarkdown %}
</div>
<div class="kh-scrolly__section" data-scrolly-steps="w w.1 w.2" data-scrolly-short>
{% markdown %}

## Why isn't this a solved problem?

**Storybook and Figma only look as if they do the same job.** Both show a design system's components, so it seems they should stay in step on their own. They don't, for the same reason a Windows app doesn't simply run on a Mac: each platform lays things out and behaves in its own way, and Figma's Auto Layout is not CSS.

**There is no standard way across.** Every system has its own components, brands and edge cases, so any conversion depends on what you start from. Most teams settle for [a degree of parity](https://designsystemdiaries.substack.com/p/how-close-should-figma-be-to-code) between the two, or accept that [the front end is the real source of truth](https://www.robinrendle.com/notes/an-incoherent-rant-about-design-systems) and update Figma by hand to keep up.

**Hard, but not impossible.** Separate what a component is from how it looks and how it is laid out, and most of the port becomes mechanical. That is also where AI helps. The work is reverse engineering: rebuilding something that already exists in another platform's terms, with the original there to check every result against.

{% endmarkdown %}
</div>
<div class="kh-scrolly__section" data-scrolly-steps="1 1.1 1.2">
{% markdown %}

## Where did the truth come from?

**The team had done the hard part already, for a different reason.** UNDRR syndicates content across its websites, which only works when every site agrees what a card, a hero or a landing page is *made of*: which fields, which are optional, how long they can run. That is structure, not appearance, and it was already settled by the time a Figma kit came up. (I've written about [Mangrove as a content architecture that happens to ship a design system](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/).)

{% endmarkdown %}
<aside class="kh-scrolly__define" data-define="nS"><p><strong><dfn>Content schema</dfn>:</strong> the agreed list of what each piece of content contains and its limits. A Book Card always has a title and a link, may have an image, and its summary has a maximum length. Agreeing this is what lets UNDRR share content across its sites.</p></aside>
{% markdown %}

**The brand settings are a second, independent source.** I merged the design tokens from UNDRR, PreventionWeb, MCR2030, DELTA Resilience and IRP into one set, which the plugin turns into 540 Figma variables. 471 of them (87.2%) are the same in every brand; the other 69 are where the brands differ: colour, corner radius, a softer surface here, hero spacing there.

{% endmarkdown %}
<aside class="kh-scrolly__define" data-define="nT"><p><strong><dfn>Design token</dfn>:</strong> a named brand setting, like <em>this blue</em>, <em>this corner roundness</em> or <em>this spacing</em>, stored once. Each brand gives the same name its own value; change one and everything using it updates.</p></aside>
{% markdown %}

**Neither source knows about the other.** A schema says nothing about colour, and a token says nothing about whether a card has an image. So you can hold one still and swap the other: below, the same Book Card schema under each brand's tokens.

{% endmarkdown %}
